            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "change passes a value on only when it differs from the one before it.\n\nA stream that repeats the same number frame after frame comes out as one value per change: 3, 3, 3, 4, 4, 2 comes out as 3, 4, 2. What comes out is the value itself, not true or false. The first value always passes - there is nothing before it for it to equal.\n\nIn the demo, a ramp is cut into whole-number steps by floor - the staircase on the plot - so it sends the same number for many frames in a row. change lets one value through per step, so the counter beside it counts steps, not frames. Below, the change node has force_int checked: drag the number slowly, and a value gets through only when its whole-number part changes.\n\nUse it to stop a slow or expensive part of a patch being retriggered by a stream that has not moved: a menu index, a scene number, a rounded sensor reading.\n\nSYNTAX:\nchange\n\nEXAMPLE:\nchange\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe value to be tested, and passed on if it succeeds. Receiving data here triggers the node.\n\ncomparison (the first menu under the node):\nThe test: !=, ==, >, >=, <, <= or always. The node's name sets it (!= here);\nyou can change it by hand, which turns this node into any of its siblings.\n\noperand (under the menu):\nWhat the value is compared with. While self_compare is checked this is the previous value, filled in after each one arrives - it shows what the next value will be compared with.\n\nself_compare:\nChecked (the default for this node): compare each value with the one before it.\nUnchecked: compare with a fixed operand you set yourself, making a threshold gate.\n\nforce_int:\nWhen checked, both sides are rounded to whole numbers before the comparison. It affects only the test - what comes out is the value as it arrived.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe input, unchanged, whenever it differs from the value before it. Otherwise nothing at all is sent.\n\nNOISE:\nA fractional sensor stream almost never repeats exactly, so change passes nearly every frame of it. Round it first, or check force_int, to react only to changes that matter.\n\nARRAYS AND TENSORS:\nThe test passes if ANY element passes it, and then the whole array is sent.\n\nSEE ALSO:\nincreasing    only values that have risen\ndecreasing    only values that have fallen\npass          a gate with a fixed threshold\ntogedge       react when a toggle turns on or off",
                    "value_type": "string"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 655,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 960,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "string"
                }
            },
            "text": "change passes a value on only when it differs from the one before it.\n\nA stream that repeats the same number frame after frame comes out as one value per change: 3, 3, 3, 4, 4, 2 comes out as 3, 4, 2. What comes out is the value itself, not true or false. The first value always passes - there is nothing before it for it to equal.\n\nIn the demo, a ramp is cut into whole-number steps by floor - the staircase on the plot - so it sends the same number for many frames in a row. change lets one value through per step, so the counter beside it counts steps, not frames. Below, the change node has force_int checked: drag the number slowly, and a value gets through only when its whole-number part changes.\n\nUse it to stop a slow or expensive part of a patch being retriggered by a stream that has not moved: a menu index, a scene number, a rounded sensor reading.\n\nSYNTAX:\nchange\n\nEXAMPLE:\nchange\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe value to be tested, and passed on if it succeeds. Receiving data here triggers the node.\n\ncomparison (the first menu under the node):\nThe test: !=, ==, >, >=, <, <= or always. The node's name sets it (!= here);\nyou can change it by hand, which turns this node into any of its siblings.\n\noperand (under the menu):\nWhat the value is compared with. While self_compare is checked this is the previous value, filled in after each one arrives - it shows what the next value will be compared with.\n\nself_compare:\nChecked (the default for this node): compare each value with the one before it.\nUnchecked: compare with a fixed operand you set yourself, making a threshold gate.\n\nforce_int:\nWhen checked, both sides are rounded to whole numbers before the comparison. It affects only the test - what comes out is the value as it arrived.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe input, unchanged, whenever it differs from the value before it. Otherwise nothing at all is sent.\n\nNOISE:\nA fractional sensor stream almost never repeats exactly, so change passes nearly every frame of it. Round it first, or check force_int, to react only to changes that matter.\n\nARRAYS AND TENSORS:\nThe test passes if ANY element passes it, and then the whole array is sent.\n\nSEE ALSO:\nincreasing    only values that have risen\ndecreasing    only values that have fallen\npass          a gate with a fixed threshold\ntogedge       react when a toggle turns on or off"
        },
